Here is a Java solution to the given problem.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

Alternatively, a solution without loops/recursion can be achieved using logarithms.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        return n > 0 && (Math.log(n) / Math.log(3)) % 1 == 0;
    }
}
```

However, due to potential precision issues with the `Math.log` function, a more robust solution is:

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        return n > 0 && (Math.log10(n) / Math.log10(3)) % 1 < 1e-10;
    }
}
```

Or using a constant representing the maximum power of 3 within the integer range.

```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        return n > 0 && 1162261467 % n == 0;
    }
}
```

